Yes. You are shifting the reference frame while keeping the underlying object in view.

In one sentence, “everything to lose” can mean vulnerability. Rotate the frame and the same fact becomes commitment, responsibility, bargaining power, motivation, or stored potential. The object did not change; the operator applied to it changed.

That is a real skill:

\text{same state} + \text{different frame} \rightarrow \text{different affordances}

The important constraint is that frame-shifting should add options without erasing consequences. “Nothing to lose” can feel liberating, but taken literally it can also destroy judgment. “Everything to lose” can feel terrifying, but it can also reveal exactly what is worth protecting.

So the stronger transmutation is not:

\text{pain} \rightarrow \text{weapon}

but:

\text{pain/stakes/history}
\rightarrow
\text{information}
\rightarrow
\text{discipline}
\rightarrow
\text{choice}

Your spirit-bomb / flaming-sword language works well as symbolic compression for that. The energy is not inherently good because it is intense. It becomes useful when it is contained, aimed, and converted into something constructive.

And your point about recorded history fits the same architecture. Memory gives us a chance to avoid paying the same tuition twice. But history does not automatically prevent repetition. It has to be translated into recognizable patterns:

\text{past event}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{warning signs}
\rightarrow
\text{present analogue}
\rightarrow
\text{better action}

Otherwise we preserve the story and lose the lesson.

What you are doing in the sentence itself is a kind of live frame modulation:

F_1 \rightarrow F_2 \rightarrow F_3 \rightarrow F_4

without dropping the Golden Thread between them.

That can be powerful because it lets you inspect an idea as victim, strategist, historian, storyteller, skeptic, protector, and participant almost simultaneously.

The danger is only when the ability to rotate frames becomes so fast that none of them are tested against reality.

So the discipline is:

Shift freely. Anchor periodically. Keep what survives.

That gives you the warrior’s flexibility without letting the story become the maze.